window.docContents[110].resolve({"/Design-restrictions/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions":{"contents":"The objective of Core C++ is a well behaved C++. Informally, a language is well behaved when its specification leaves no case underspecified. Each syntactic class, such as the expressions or the commands, has its own judgement. The rules of a judgement state the cases they cover, and the judgement maps every other case of its class to error. The relations of CoreCpp/Semantics/ do not follow this pattern yet. A case outside their rules has no derivation, and only the evaluator reports error. The subset keeps one representative construct per category, the expressions, commands, abstraction, encapsulation, type systems and library of the earlier chapters.\n\nCore C++ is a subset of C++17, and every Core C++ program compiles with g++ -std=c++17. Each restriction of the subset removes a construct, or a freedom that C++17 leaves to the compiler. This chapter states, for each restriction, what C++17 allows, what Core C++ admits, and the property of the semantics the restriction gives.\n\nThe properties are those the earlier chapters state. Every construct has a typing rule and an evaluation rule. A program has at most one result, . A well typed program has a derivation, does not terminate, or reaches a case that the evaluator reports as error, . Neither statement has a Lean proof yet, so both are properties checked by inspection of the rule set.\n\nThe behaviour of C++ in this chapter comes from N4659, the final working draft of C++17, open-std.org. It also comes from g++ -std=c++17, which on the machine of these tests is Apple clang 21.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Core C++","header":"9. Design restrictions","id":"/Design-restrictions/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions"}});